      ":question/problem": "Express $\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$. Enter both signed coefficients, including zero when needed.\n\n$\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)=$ {{field-1}} $\\sin x+$ {{field-2}} $\\cos x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v)=\\sin u\\cos v+\\cos u\\sin v.\n$$\n\nThe fixed angle $\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$ is in quadrant IV with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$, then simplify the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\sin x\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)+\\cos x\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\sin x\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\cos x\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\sin x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cos x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested coefficients are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, respectively. A negative coefficient goes inside its blank.",
